WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:06.506
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.476
This is the skull of one of the most famous

00:00:18.476 --> 00:00:22.188
of dinosaurs, Tyrannosaurus rex.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:26.776
It's also one of the most intensively studied species.

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:29.988
Yet even so, new discoveries are still

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:33.116
being made about this formidable predator.

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:40.123
Something that continues to fascinate us

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:42.459
is how good they were at hunting.

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:52.302
Key factors in their success were their senses.

00:00:52.302 --> 00:01:01.811
So how powerful were their eyes, their hearing, and their sense

00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:02.353
of smell?

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:10.570
When we want to understand a little bit about dinosaur

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.865
senses, it's really, really difficult.

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:17.243
We don't have any of the sense organs preserved.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:20.663
But we can get some clues to things like eyesight,

00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:24.000
just from the outside of the skulls.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.085
We can look at the shape of the eye sockets.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:28.880
This gives us some clues as to the size of the eyeball,

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.675
and also the directions in which the animal were looking.

00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:39.516
T-rex eyes faced forwards, evidence for binocular vision.

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:43.061
And they were huge, the size of grapefruits.

00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:47.148
The fields of vision of its two eyes

00:01:47.148 --> 00:01:50.193
overlap substantially, giving it very good depth perception,

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:52.362
which would have made it a very effective predator.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:59.702
In the modern world, hunters like African wild dogs

00:01:59.702 --> 00:02:02.205
have superb vision.

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:04.624
But they don't just rely on sight.

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:10.838
Combined with their acute senses of smell and hearing,

00:02:10.838 --> 00:02:13.675
they can attract prey from miles away.

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:19.472
Could T-rex do the same?

00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:29.274
New medical scanning techniques provide evidence that it could.

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:32.402
CT scanning is something that's really revolutionized our work

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:34.320
in paleontology, and it's giving us

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.990
insights that were simply impossible to gain from looking

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.243
at the bones themselves.

00:02:40.243 --> 00:02:46.708
We can now reveal secrets hidden inside T-rex's brain.

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:48.084
What we can see is that there are

00:02:48.084 --> 00:02:50.211
key areas that relate to different senses,

00:02:50.211 --> 00:02:53.047
like eyesight and smell.

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:57.760
These are located in specific lobes of the brain.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.972
The bigger those lobes are, the better

00:03:00.972 --> 00:03:04.100
the eyesight or the better the sense of smell.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:08.604
Large optic and olfactory lobes suggest an excellent sense

00:03:08.604 --> 00:03:11.482
of sight and smell.

00:03:11.482 --> 00:03:14.360
But it doesn't end there.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.946
The inner ear is adapted to pick up

00:03:16.946 --> 00:03:23.661
low frequencies, an advantage when detecting

00:03:23.661 --> 00:03:27.707
prey over long distances.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.709
These really impressive senses would

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:32.545
have combined to allow them to hone in on prey very

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:36.966
quickly and very accurately.

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:39.969
Now, there's an exciting new theory.

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:47.685
Night vision cameras have revealed many modern predators,

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:52.857
like lions, can use their highly developed senses to hunt

00:03:52.857 --> 00:03:53.357
at night.

00:03:56.861 --> 00:03:59.155
Perhaps T-rex could do this, too.

00:04:02.366 --> 00:04:03.868
Until relatively recently, it was

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:08.623
assumed that dinosaurs were mostly animals of the daytime.

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:11.542
But if we actually look at the behavior of living animals,

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:14.045
there's no reason to think this is true.

00:04:20.843 --> 00:04:23.387
Predators can use the cover of darkness

00:04:23.387 --> 00:04:28.309
to ambush other animals much more easily.

00:04:28.309 --> 00:04:30.269
It's likely T-rex would have made

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:33.773
the most of such an advantage.

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:36.901
Their very, very powerful sense of sight and smell

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:40.238
would have allowed them to hunt prey very, very effectively

00:04:40.238 --> 00:04:43.241
in low light conditions, and perhaps even at night.

00:04:47.161 --> 00:04:51.332
Modern research techniques mean scientists continue

00:04:51.332 --> 00:04:55.211
to make new and surprising discoveries, not only about

00:04:55.211 --> 00:05:00.299
T-rex, but many of the other dinosaurs

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:03.678
that lived on our prehistoric planet.

